Here's how to set up switchable branded themes in .NET MAUI:

## 1. Define Theme Resource Dictionaries

Create a separate `ResourceDictionary` for each theme:

**Themes/LightTheme.xaml**
```xml
<ResourceDictionary xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/dotnet/2021/maui"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2009/xaml"
                    x:Class="MyApp.Themes.LightTheme">
    <Color x:Key="PageBackground">#FFFFFF</Color>
    <Color x:Key="TextPrimary">#1A1A1A</Color>
    <Color x:Key="AccentColor">#0078D4</Color>
</ResourceDictionary>
```

**Themes/DarkTheme.xaml** and **Themes/HighContrastTheme.xaml** — same keys, different values.

## 2. Create a Theme Service

```csharp
public enum AppTheme { Light, Dark, HighContrast }

public static class ThemeService
{
    public static void ApplyTheme(AppTheme theme)
    {
        var mergedDicts = Application.Current.Resources.MergedDictionaries;
        mergedDicts.Clear();

        mergedDicts.Add(theme switch
        {
            AppTheme.Dark => new Themes.DarkTheme(),
            AppTheme.HighContrast => new Themes.HighContrastTheme(),
            _ => new Themes.LightTheme()
        });

        // Persist choice
        Preferences.Set("AppTheme", theme.ToString());
    }

    public static void LoadSavedTheme()
    {
        var saved = Preferences.Get("AppTheme", nameof(AppTheme.Light));
        if (Enum.TryParse<AppTheme>(saved, out var theme))
            ApplyTheme(theme);
    }
}
```

## 3. Apply on Startup

In `App.xaml.cs`:
```csharp
public App()
{
    InitializeComponent();
    ThemeService.LoadSavedTheme();
    MainPage = new AppShell();
}
```

## 4. Settings Page

```xml
<Picker Title="Theme" SelectedIndexChanged="OnThemeChanged">
    <Picker.ItemsSource>
        <x:Array Type="{x:Type x:String}">
            <x:String>Light</x:String>
            <x:String>Dark</x:String>
            <x:String>High Contrast</x:String>
        </x:Array>
    </Picker.ItemsSource>
</Picker>
```

```csharp
void OnThemeChanged(object sender, EventArgs e)
{
    var picker = (Picker)sender;
    var theme = picker.SelectedIndex switch
    {
        1 => AppTheme.Dark,
        2 => AppTheme.HighContrast,
        _ => AppTheme.Light
    };
    ThemeService.ApplyTheme(theme);
}
```

## 5. Use Theme Resources in Your Views

Reference keys with `DynamicResource` (not `StaticResource`) so changes propagate immediately:

```xml
<ContentPage BackgroundColor="{DynamicResource PageBackground}">
    <Label TextColor="{DynamicResource TextPrimary}" Text="Hello!" />
</ContentPage>
```

**Key points:**
- Use `DynamicResource` everywhere — it re-evaluates when the dictionary changes.
- Keep identical keys across all theme dictionaries.
- `Preferences` persists the selection across app restarts.
